Hi Dennis,
And thanks for the explicative email.

Dennis Murphy wrote:
> Hi Lorenzo:
>
> There are differences in scale between a frequency histogram and a
> 'density' histogram.
> In the latter, the expectation is that the total area encompassed by
> the histogram will be
> (close to) 1; there is no such restriction in the former. By
> specifying a density histogram,
> you've changed the scale on the vertical axis, in this case to
> effectively between  0 and 0.8.
> Do you really want to represent values in that range on a log scale?

Yes, or let us say I would like to represent the interval [y_min>0, 0.8]
on a log scale. More about what I mean by y_min in the following.

> That's how you got
> the inverted histogram in the first place: it was using log10(1) = 0
> as the reference point for
> drawing the graph. I'm pretty sure you're not asking for -infinity on
> the lower end of the
> y-scale, right?.  I don't understand your question about 'wanting the
> y-axis
> to stretch high enough to include all my rescaled bins'. Do you want
> it *just* high enough
> or do you want something else? If the former, how do you want the tick
> values to be spaced?
>

I want the tick values to be equally logarithmically spaced . Perhaps I
should make clear what I have in mind.
Let us say you have many data (contact durations) delta_t distributed as
a power-law
P(delta_t)=K*delta_t^(-gamma), K and gamma constants.
If you plot the frequency (or number of counts, overall normalization
factors do not matter here) of each value of delta_t vs delta_t on a
bi-logarithmic scale (regardless of the way you normalize the data),
then you see (more or less) that they fall along a line.
See e.g. http://www.hpl.hp.com/research/idl/papers/ranking/ranking.html .
Being the data pretty noisy in my case, one would like to group them
into a lognormally spaced bin structure and generate what I would
loosely and incorrectly call a density histogram on a log-log scale. So,
on the y axis I would like to read powers of 10.
Of course, the y axis cannot start from 0 (i.e. -infinity on a log
scale) but should span e.g. [y_min,y_max], an interval to be represented
as powers of 10, where y_min is a nonzero value below the density in the
each non-empty bin and y_max is a value above the density in each
non-empty bin.
I hope that this clarifies what I am after.
Cheers

Lorenzo

> Note that the shape of the histogram will be the same whether it is a
> frequency,
> percent/relative frequency or density histogram. The only thing that
> will change is the
> representation of values on the vertical axis, and that represents
> differences in scale.
> I'm sure you know this, but we have to be clear about the meaning and
> representation of
> the vertical axis between different manifestations of a histogram.
>
> HTH,
