Dennis Murphy wrote:
> Hi Lorenzo:
>
> There are differences in scale between a frequency histogram and a
> 'density' histogram.
> In the latter, the expectation is that the total area encompassed by
> the histogram will be
> (close to) 1; there is no such restriction in the former. By
> specifying a density histogram,
> you've changed the scale on the vertical axis, in this case to
> effectively between  0 and 0.8.
> Do you really want to represent values in that range on a log scale?
> That's how you got
> the inverted histogram in the first place: it was using log10(1) = 0
> as the reference point for
> drawing the graph. I'm pretty sure you're not asking for -infinity on
> the lower end of the
> y-scale, right?.  I don't understand your question about 'wanting the
> y-axis
> to stretch high enough to include all my rescaled bins'. Do you want
> it *just* high enough
> or do you want something else? If the former, how do you want the tick
> values to be spaced?
>
> Note that the shape of the histogram will be the same whether it is a
> frequency,
> percent/relative frequency or density histogram. The only thing that
> will change is the
> representation of values on the vertical axis, and that represents
> differences in scale.
> I'm sure you know this, but we have to be clear about the meaning and
> representation of
> the vertical axis between different manifestations of a histogram.
>
> HTH,
> Dennis
