Hi Y'all,
Unfortunately there is no "the" answer. One way would be to write
an engine that would take a grob tree and then map tags to a set of
elements
in the tree. Then an image function could create a png and an image map
from the modified object tree. Another approach, more work, less adhock
would be to write a mapping between grid elements and javascript
elements,
so that basically you would be writing a javascript device. It really
depends
on how much of the plot you need the user to interact with and where you
want
the computation to happen. With the image map route, if you want the
plot to redraw
when the canvas area is resized the whole command has to go back to the
sever,
redraw and resend, yuck. The closest existing solution would be to use
the svgdevice
and then process the xml generated. OR use something like dojo's svg
importer
and use their plotting tools to process and tag the elements in the
browser.
All solutions have a substantial computational cost, and require careful
thought about
where computation should take place, client or server. Also care needs
to be taken, as none of this scales very well.

My 2c

Nicholas

